He's sort of the natural heir to Jonathan Swift of Gulliver's Travels
fame; dry Brit humor with barely disguised digs at everything that makes
western civilization so completely unworkable.  It is wrapped up in a
fantasy scene, and full of great puns; one story is about "Music with
Rocks" for example, though it may be you have to bump into the puns in
situo to get the full impact.

I read email or news one time from a fellow who described sitting with
a group talking in a bar with Pratchett; my correspondent said the ideas
just flew off Pratchett like electric sparks, the guy just couldn't keep
them inside for a moment.

Discworld is his most popular stuff, and if I were starting over, I'd
still read it in _as published_ order, because the jokes build on each
other from volume to volume, especially for the "minor" characters like
Cut Me Own Throat Dibbler and Carrot, the latter of whom has now had
entire novels written around him.

I look at his completely ineffectual wizard Rincewind, and I see me
trying to survive to level 3 in a robe; why he isn't in NetHack I
haven't a clue.  Rincewind is an inspiration to us all, however, he
seems to have the concept of putting one foot in front of another in
the direction away from danger down to a negative reaction time basis.

There are also, if you have some literate pre-teens to gift, an almost
equally excellent series of books aimed at the ten to twelve year old
crowd, I can remember Truckers, Diggers, and The Carpet People, but
there are a bunch more; they are hard to find in the US, but probably
easy from UK book dealers.

xanthian.

Another whole untapped mythos for NetHack would be Glen Cook's fantasy
detective series, gritty and violent and very, very funny. I want
Saucerhead Tharp as my personal assistant in the Dungeons of Doom.


